BOUNDARY AND PRE-FREEZE ASSESSMENT
All five terminal paths were inspected before seal. Positive requires complete
drain, natural producer0/null and consumer0/null. Producer-exit7 requires complete
drain, producer7/null and exact STREAM_PROCESS forwarding. Consumer-failure keeps
the original v3.1 identity-bound EPIPE alternative only. Timeout and allocation
both previously threw before the core catch could signal, letting iterator cleanup
close stdout first. Strict producer SIGTERM therefore required controlled ordering.

The separate worker-local hook retains the real ChildProcess, signals SIGTERM
while consumption is blocked and its stdout is still open, awaits actual process
exit, then destroys the owned pipe and awaits child close before throwing the
original boundary Error object. Waiting for child close with unread touched stdout
still open can deadlock; the pinned Node internal/child_process and readable
source inspections are recorded in PREFLIGHT.json and recipe/STATIC-FINAL.json.
Both stages share the unchanged3000ms cleanup grace. No core or producer change;
no global EPIPE handling, fabricated SIGTERM, or timeout predicate waiver.

The supervisor now bounds final closure after the unchanged45000ms deadline and
two existing3000ms grace windows. Native ps calls retain their10000ms bounds;
synchronous host work can delay timer dispatch, so this is not real-time assurance.
It does not signal a recorded post-exit group leader PID. Unproved reaping or lost
ownership is unsafe STOP, not assumed cleanup. This run needed no emergency for
the real controls. The intentionally hanging allocation consumer was actually
killed only after its numeric receipt and producer close were durable.

